# Binary matrices for the Parrott words

These 52-dimensional binary matrices use the four simple coroots first,
then the 24 positive roots in `FixedCoordinates.RootPartition.table`, then
those roots' negatives in the same order. They are the candidate values of
`Realization.assignment`: the pair blocks are P₁,P₃,P₂,P₀ and the quartet
blocks are Q₀,Q₃,Q₁,Q₂, with parameters (1,1,0,1). The Weyl words are
[1,2,1,2] and [0,3]. The separate matrix comparison must prove that these
literal arrays are the values of the actual Ree words; their definition does
not assert that comparison. `BinaryRelations` checks the presentation directly.

Source: Parrott (1972), printed p.683, with the commutator a⁻¹b⁻¹ab.
The generic packed-matrix checker is reused from `Thompson.BinaryMatrix`;
no Thompson model or group theorem is used.
-/

